What about {lo pendo ba ca'a pendo}?  It seems to carry the implication
"Friends will be *true* friends" (whatever that means) without resorting to
too many words.  Maybe even {lo pendo ca'a pendo}, leaving out the temporal
tense.

In that vein, {jetnu xu fa lo du'u lo nu'o broda cu broda .ibo go'i xu lo
du'u lo broda nu'o broda}

On Fri, Feb 4, 2011 at 5:58 PM, Remo Dentato <rdentato@gmail.com> wrote:

> Actually I always interpreted the line "Friends will be friends" in that
> song as "Real friends will remain friends forever".
> So it's not just the tautology that "those who are friends will behave as
> friends do" but more the fact that you can rely on the fact that they will
> continue to behave that way in the future (possibly when you will need
> them).
>
> {lo pendo ba pendo} seems good to me. {lo ca pendo ba pendo} seems too
> pedantic.
